{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 27, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades and a powerful ruler in the Levant during a turbulent period. His reign was marked by both military campaigns and diplomatic maneuvering aimed at securing and expanding his territories.\n\n*Early Life and Accession*\n\nRaymond was born around 1120 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Countess Maria of Antioch. He inherited the County of Tripoli upon his father's death in 1154. His early years were spent consolidating his power dealing with internal rebellions and external threats particularly from Muslim forces. He faced challenges from rival claimants to the county and worked to maintain stability within his domain. He also engaged in diplomatic efforts to strengthen alliances with other Crusader states.\n\nRaymond III actively participated in the Third Crusade. He provided crucial military support to King Richard the Lionheart and other Crusaders. His forces played a vital role in the siege of Acre a major strategic objective of the Crusade. He demonstrated military prowess and earned a reputation as a skilled commander. His involvement in the Third Crusade solidified his position as a prominent Crusader leader.\n\n*Reign and Military Campaigns*\n\nThroughout his reign Raymond III faced constant pressure from Muslim rulers. He waged numerous campaigns against the Zengids and other enemies seeking to maintain control over Tripoli and its surrounding territories. He engaged in a complex web of alliances and rivalries with neighboring Crusader states such as Antioch and Jerusalem. His diplomacy was often as important as his military strength in navigating the political landscape of the Levant. He rebuilt fortifications and strengthened the defenses of Tripoli to withstand attacks. He also fostered economic growth within the county promoting trade and agriculture.\n\nRaymond III’s military campaigns extended beyond direct attacks on Muslim territories. He also engaged in expeditions to secure coastal regions and disrupt Muslim shipping. He understood the importance of naval power and invested in building a fleet to protect Tripoli’s trade routes. His reign was characterized by a constant struggle for survival and a determined effort to maintain Crusader presence in the region.\n\n*Legacy and Death*\n\nRaymond III died in 1199 and was succeeded by his nephew Raymond IV. His reign is remembered as a period of relative stability and prosperity for the County of Tripoli amidst ongoing warfare. He left behind a strengthened county with improved defenses and a robust economy. He is regarded as a capable ruler and a skilled military commander who played a significant role in the Crusades. His diplomatic efforts and military campaigns helped to preserve Crusader presence in the Levant for several decades. His legacy is one of resilience and determination in the face of adversity. He is often depicted as a pragmatic and resourceful leader who prioritized the security and well-being of his people.\n\n\n\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0021344278939068317}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.50339189733495e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2371193766593933}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.20648594200611115}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.054002758115530014}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁from", "logprob": -0.0001867835089797154}, {"token": "▁", "logprob": -2.8490614567999728e-05}, {"token": "1", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "1", "logprob": -8.4638240878121e-06}, {"token": "5", "logprob": -2.3841574147809297e-05}, {"token": "4", "logprob": -0.0013188959565013647}, {"token": "▁until",